<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>

<section xml:id="sec-continuous-probability-ii" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
    <title>Continuous Probability II</title>
    <objectives component="outcomes">
        <introduction>
            <p>
            <dl>
                <li>
                    <title>Strand</title>
                    <p>3.0 Statistics and Probability</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Sub-Strand</title>
                    <p>3.2 Probability II</p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <title>Specific Learning Outcomes</title>
                    <p>Determine discrete and continuous probabilities in different situations</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Grade Level</title>
                    <p>11</p>
                </li>
                <li component="lesson-plan">
                    <title>Estimated Duration</title>
                    <p>40 minutes</p>
                </li>
            </dl>
            </p>
        </introduction>
    </objectives>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Problem-Solving and Discovery</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    Learners extend the principle <q>probability = area</q> from the previous section (uniform distribution) to a circular region, discovering that the shape of the favourable region changes the calculation but not the underlying idea.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Do not introduce any new distribution yet. Let learners compute area ÷ total area freely. After part (c), ask: <q>The answer involves π. Where did that come from? What would the answer be if the region were a triangle instead?</q> The goal is for learners to see that the probability principle is universal — only the geometry changes.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <activity xml:id="act-chicken-in-yard">
        <title>The Chicken in the Yard</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                A chicken wanders randomly around a 10 m × 10 m square yard. It is equally likely to be at any point in the yard at any moment.
            </p>
            <p>
                The farmer has drawn a circle of radius 5 m centred in the middle of the yard. There is also a small rectangular corner pen bounded by <m>7 \leq x \leq 10</m> and <m>7 \leq y \leq 10</m> (in metres from one corner of the yard).
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-chicken-yard">
                <caption>The 10 m × 10 m yard (gray), the circular region of radius 5 m (blue), and the corner pen (orange).</caption>
                <image width="40%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.42]
                            % Yard background
                            \fill[gray!15] (0,0) rectangle (10,10);
                            \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (10,10);
                            % Circle (radius 5, centre (5,5))
                            \fill[blue!20] (5,5) circle (5);
                            \draw[blue, thick] (5,5) circle (5);
                            % Corner pen (7-10, 7-10)
                            \fill[orange!40] (7,7) rectangle (10,10);
                            \draw[orange!80!black, thick] (7,7) rectangle (10,10);
                            % Labels
                            \node[blue!70!black, font=\small] at (5,4) {circle};
                            \node[orange!80!black, font=\scriptsize] at (8.5,8.5) {corner};
                            % Dimension labels
                            \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (5,-0.4) {10 m};
                            \node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (-0.4,5) {10 m};
                            % Radius marker
                            \draw[gray, thin] (5,5) -- (10,5);
                            \node[above, font=\scriptsize] at (7.5,5.1) {5 m};
                            \fill (5,5) circle (0.12);
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the total area of the yard?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p><m>10 \times 10 = 100 \text{ m}^2</m>.</p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>What is the area of the circular region?</p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p><m>\pi \times 5^2 = 25\pi \approx 78.5 \text{ m}^2</m>.</p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Since the chicken is equally likely to be anywhere, what is the probability that at a random moment the chicken is inside the circle?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                <m>P(\text{inside circle}) = \dfrac{25\pi}{100} = \dfrac{\pi}{4} \approx 0.785</m>.
                                Note the answer involves <m>\pi</m> — because the favourable region is circular.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            What is the probability the chicken is inside the rectangular corner pen?
                        </p>
                        <solution component="lesson-plan">
                            <p>
                                Area of pen = <m>3 \times 3 = 9 \text{ m}^2</m>.
                                <m>P(\text{in corner}) = \dfrac{9}{100} = 0.09</m>.
                                This is back to a rectangle — no <m>\pi</m> needed. The method is always the same: area of favourable region divided by total area.
                            </p>
                        </solution>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
    </activity>

    <axiom component="lesson-plan">
        <title>Structured Instruction</title>
        <dl>
            <li>
                <title>Suggested Time</title>
                <p>12 minutes</p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Objective</title>
                <p>
                    To introduce the normal distribution (bell curve) and the 68–95–99.7 rule, and to briefly present the exponential distribution as a formula-based tool for waiting times.
                </p>
            </li>
            <li>
                <title>Teacher's Role</title>
                <p>
                    Connect to the activity: <q>The circle gave us a non-uniform shape. In practice, many measurements — heights, weights, exam scores — cluster around a middle value in a bell-shaped curve. That bell shape is the normal distribution.</q>
                </p>
                <p>
                    Write <m>X \sim N(\mu, \sigma)</m> on the board. Explain: <m>\mu</m> is the mean (where the bell peaks), <m>\sigma</m> is the standard deviation (how spread out the bell is). Draw a rough bell curve and mark the three bands:
                    <ul>
                        <li><p>68% of values fall within 1<m>\sigma</m> of <m>\mu</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>95% of values fall within 2<m>\sigma</m> of <m>\mu</m></p></li>
                        <li><p>99.7% of values fall within 3<m>\sigma</m> of <m>\mu</m></p></li>
                    </ul>
                    Emphasise symmetry: since the curve is symmetric about <m>\mu</m>, we can find one-sided probabilities by halving. For example, <m>P(X &gt; \mu + \sigma) = \frac{1 - 0.68}{2} = 0.16</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Briefly introduce the exponential distribution for waiting times. State the formula <m>P(X &lt; x) = 1 - e^{-\lambda x}</m> without derivation. Learners are expected to substitute numbers into this formula, not derive it.
                </p>
            </li>
        </dl>
    </axiom>

    <insight>
        <title>Key Ideas</title>
        <p>
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Geometric probability</term>: When a point is chosen at random in a region, the probability it falls in a subregion is:
                        <md>P = \frac{\text{area of favourable region}}{\text{total area}}.</md>
                        The calculation depends on the shape of the region — rectangles, circles, triangles — and uses the appropriate area formula.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>normal distribution</term> <m>N(\mu, \sigma)</m> is a symmetric bell-shaped distribution with mean <m>\mu</m> and standard deviation <m>\sigma</m>. It describes many natural measurements such as heights, weights, and test scores.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>Symmetry:</term> The normal curve is symmetric about <m>\mu</m>, so <m>P(X &gt; \mu) = P(X &lt; \mu) = 0.5</m>.
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        <term>68–95–99.7 rule</term> (empirical rule):
                        <md>
                            P(\mu - \sigma &lt; X &lt; \mu + \sigma) \approx 0.68\\
                            P(\mu - 2\sigma &lt; X &lt; \mu + 2\sigma) \approx 0.95\\
                            P(\mu - 3\sigma &lt; X &lt; \mu + 3\sigma) \approx 0.997
                        </md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        Combining symmetry with the rule gives one-sided probabilities. For example:
                        <md>P(X &gt; \mu + \sigma) = \frac{1 - 0.68}{2} = 0.16, \qquad P(X &gt; \mu + 2\sigma) = \frac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025.</md>
                    </p>
                </li>
                <li>
                    <p>
                        The <term>exponential distribution</term> <m>\text{Exp}(\lambda)</m> models the waiting time until the next random event, where events occur at rate <m>\lambda</m> per unit time. The mean waiting time is <m>1/\lambda</m>. The cumulative probability formula is:
                        <md>P(X &lt; x) = 1 - e^{-\lambda x} \quad \text{for } x \geq 0.</md>
                        <em>(This formula is given — no derivation is required.)</em>
                    </p>
                </li>
            </ul>
        </p>
    </insight>

    <figure xml:id="fig-normal-bell-curve">
        <caption>
            The bell curve of a normal distribution <m>N(\mu, \sigma)</m>. The three shaded bands show the 68–95–99.7 rule: 68% of values fall within <m>\mu \pm \sigma</m> (darkest), 95% within <m>\mu \pm 2\sigma</m> (medium), and 99.7% within <m>\mu \pm 3\sigma</m> (lightest).
        </caption>
        <image width="72%">
            <latex-image>
                \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.2]
                    % Gaussian: f(x) = 4 * exp(-x^2/2) / sqrt(2*pi); sqrt(2*pi) ~ 2.5066
                    % 99.7% band: -3 to 3 (lightest)
                    \fill[blue!15, domain=-3:3, smooth, samples=60]
                        plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066}) -- (3,0) -- (-3,0) -- cycle;
                    % 95% band: -2 to 2 (medium)
                    \fill[blue!35, domain=-2:2, smooth, samples=40]
                        plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066}) -- (2,0) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
                    % 68% band: -1 to 1 (darkest)
                    \fill[blue!60, domain=-1:1, smooth, samples=20]
                        plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066}) -- (1,0) -- (-1,0) -- cycle;
                    % Bell curve outline
                    \draw[blue!80!black, thick, domain=-3.6:3.6, smooth, samples=80]
                        plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066});
                    % Dashed verticals at ±sigma and ±2sigma
                    \foreach \xp in {-2,-1,1,2}
                        \draw[dashed, gray!70] (\xp,0) -- (\xp,{4*exp(-\xp*\xp/2)/2.5066});
                    % Axes
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-4.2,0) -- (4.2,0) node[right] {$x$};
                    \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.25) -- (0,1.85) node[above] {$f(x)$};
                    % x-axis tick marks
                    \foreach \xp in {-3,-2,-1,0,1,2,3}
                        \draw (\xp,0.05) -- (\xp,-0.05);
                    % x-axis labels
                    \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-2,-0.05) {$\mu{-}2\sigma$};
                    \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (-1,-0.05) {$\mu{-}\sigma$};
                    \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-0.08) {$\mu$};
                    \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (1,-0.05) {$\mu{+}\sigma$};
                    \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (2,-0.05) {$\mu{+}2\sigma$};
                    % Band labels
                    \node[white, font=\small\bfseries] at (0,0.22) {68\%};
                    \node[font=\small] at (1.55,0.12) {95\%};
                    \node[font=\scriptsize] at (2.75,0.05) {99.7\%};
                \end{tikzpicture}
            </latex-image>
        </image>
    </figure>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/example-intro.ptx" />

    <example>
        <title>Applying the 68–95–99.7 Rule</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                The heights of Form 3 boys in a Kenya school are normally distributed with mean 165 cm and standard deviation 8 cm. Let <m>X \sim N(165, 8)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(157 &lt; X &lt; 173)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 181)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>In a group of 500 boys, how many would you expect to have height between 149 cm and 181 cm?</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here <m>\mu = 165</m> and <m>\sigma = 8</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Note that <m>157 = 165 - 8 = \mu - \sigma</m> and <m>173 = 165 + 8 = \mu + \sigma</m>. By the 68–95–99.7 rule:
                            <md>P(157 &lt; X &lt; 173) = P(\mu - \sigma &lt; X &lt; \mu + \sigma) \approx 0.68.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Note that <m>181 = 165 + 2(8) = \mu + 2\sigma</m>. The 95% rule gives <m>P(\mu - 2\sigma &lt; X &lt; \mu + 2\sigma) = 0.95</m>, so by symmetry:
                            <md>P(X &gt; 181) = P(X &gt; \mu + 2\sigma) = \frac{1 - 0.95}{2} = 0.025.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            Note <m>149 = \mu - 2\sigma</m> and <m>181 = \mu + 2\sigma</m>, so <m>P(149 &lt; X &lt; 181) \approx 0.95</m>.
                            <md>\text{Expected count} = 0.95 \times 500 = 475 \text{ boys}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Using Symmetry for Non-Centred Intervals</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Marks in a national test follow <m>X \sim N(60, 10)</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 70)</m>.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find <m>P(40 &lt; X &lt; 70)</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here <m>\mu = 60</m> and <m>\sigma = 10</m>, so <m>\mu + \sigma = 70</m> and <m>\mu - 2\sigma = 40</m>.
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-normal-n60-10">
                <caption>
                    The bell curve <m>N(60, 10)</m> with the interval <m>(40, 70)</m> shaded. The lighter region shows <m>P(40 &lt; X &lt; 60) = 0.475</m> (left half of the 95% band); the darker region shows <m>P(60 &lt; X &lt; 70) = 0.34</m> (right half of the 68% band).
                </caption>
                <image width="65%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
                            % Gaussian: 4*exp(-x^2/2)/2.5066 (sigma units centred at 0)
                            % Shade left region x=-2 to 0 (light blue, 0.475)
                            \fill[blue!25, domain=-2:0, smooth, samples=30]
                                plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066}) -- (0,0) -- (-2,0) -- cycle;
                            % Shade right region x=0 to 1 (medium blue, 0.34)
                            \fill[blue!55, domain=0:1, smooth, samples=15]
                                plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066}) -- (1,0) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
                            % Bell curve outline
                            \draw[blue!80!black, thick, domain=-3.6:3.6, smooth, samples=80]
                                plot (\x, {4*exp(-\x*\x/2)/2.5066});
                            % Dashed verticals at x=-2 (40), x=0 (60), x=1 (70)
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (-2,0) -- (-2,{4*exp(-2)/2.5066});
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (0,0) -- (0,{4/2.5066});
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (1,0) -- (1,{4*exp(-0.5)/2.5066});
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-4,0) -- (4,0) node[right] {$x$};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,1.85) node[above] {$f(x)$};
                            % x-axis labels (actual values for N(60,10))
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (-2,-0.07) {40};
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (0,-0.07) {60};
                            \node[below, font=\small] at (1,-0.07) {70};
                            % Region labels
                            \node[font=\scriptsize] at (-1,0.18) {0.475};
                            \node[white, font=\scriptsize\bfseries] at (0.5,0.13) {0.34};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <m>70 = \mu + \sigma</m>, so by symmetry:
                            <md>P(X &gt; 70) = P(X &gt; \mu + \sigma) = \frac{1 - 0.68}{2} = 0.16.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The interval <m>(40, 70)</m> is not symmetric about <m>\mu = 60</m>, so we split it at the mean:
                            <md>
                                P(40 &lt; X &lt; 70) = P(40 &lt; X &lt; 60) + P(60 &lt; X &lt; 70).
                            </md>
                            By symmetry, <m>P(40 &lt; X &lt; 60) = \frac{0.95}{2} = 0.475</m> and <m>P(60 &lt; X &lt; 70) = \frac{0.68}{2} = 0.34</m>. Therefore:
                            <md>P(40 &lt; X &lt; 70) = 0.475 + 0.34 = 0.815.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>Working Backwards from a Probability</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Daily milk yield from a herd of dairy cows is <m>X \sim N(20, 3)</m> litres.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the interval that contains the central 95% of cows.</p></li>
                    <li><p>A farmer says the top 16% of cows yield more than <m>m</m> litres per day. Find <m>m</m>.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                Here <m>\mu = 20</m> and <m>\sigma = 3</m>.
            </p>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            The central 95% lies within <m>\mu \pm 2\sigma = 20 \pm 6</m>, giving the interval <m>(14, 26)</m> litres.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            We need <m>P(X &gt; m) = 0.16</m>. From the rule, <m>P(X &gt; \mu + \sigma) = \frac{1-0.68}{2} = 0.16</m>. Therefore:
                            <md>m = \mu + \sigma = 20 + 3 = 23 \text{ litres per day}.</md>
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <example>
        <title>The Exponential Distribution — Waiting Times</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                At a busy matatu terminus, matatus depart on average every 10 minutes. The waiting time <m>X</m> (in minutes) until the next departure follows an exponential distribution with <m>\lambda = 0.1</m>, so the mean waiting time is <m>\frac{1}{0.1} = 10</m> minutes. The cumulative probability is:
                <md>P(X &lt; x) = 1 - e^{-0.1x}.</md>
            </p>
            <figure xml:id="fig-exp-matatu">
                <caption>
                    The PDF of waiting time <m>X \sim \mathrm{Exp}(0.1)</m>. The shaded region represents <m>P(X &lt; 5) \approx 0.393</m>.
                </caption>
                <image width="58%">
                    <latex-image>
                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.1]
                            % 1 tikz unit = 5 minutes; PDF scaled: 0.8*exp(-0.5*t)
                            % Shaded: P(X &lt; 5) = region from t=0 to t=1
                            \fill[blue!30, domain=0:1, smooth, samples=20]
                                plot (\x, {0.8*exp(-0.5*\x)}) -- (1,0) -- (0,0) -- cycle;
                            % Exponential PDF curve
                            \draw[blue!80!black, thick, domain=0:5.2, smooth, samples=60]
                                plot (\x, {0.8*exp(-0.5*\x)});
                            % Dashed vertical at t=1 (5 min)
                            \draw[dashed, gray] (1,0) -- (1,{0.8*exp(-0.5)});
                            % Axes
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-0.2,0) -- (5.8,0) node[right] {$t$ (min)};
                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.1) -- (0,1.05) node[above] {$f(t)$};
                            % Ticks at 1=5min, 2=10min, 3=15min, 4=20min
                            \draw (1,0.05) -- (1,-0.05) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {5};
                            \draw (2,0.05) -- (2,-0.05) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {10};
                            \draw (3,0.05) -- (3,-0.05) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {15};
                            \draw (4,0.05) -- (4,-0.05) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {20};
                            \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (0,-0.05) {0};
                            % Label inside shaded region
                            \node[font=\scriptsize] at (0.48,0.12) {$\approx\!39\%$};
                        \end{tikzpicture}
                    </latex-image>
                </image>
            </figure>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li><p>Find the probability of waiting less than 5 minutes.</p></li>
                    <li><p>Find the probability of waiting more than 20 minutes.</p></li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <ol marker="(a)">
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X &lt; 5) = 1 - e^{-0.1 \times 5} = 1 - e^{-0.5} \approx 1 - 0.607 = 0.393.</md>
                            There is approximately a 39% chance of waiting fewer than 5 minutes.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                    <li>
                        <p>
                            <md>P(X &gt; 20) = 1 - P(X &lt; 20) = 1 - (1 - e^{-0.1 \times 20}) = e^{-2} \approx 0.135.</md>
                            There is approximately a 13.5% chance of waiting more than 20 minutes.
                        </p>
                    </li>
                </ol>
            </p>
        </solution>
    </example>

    <xi:include href="../lesson-plan-templates/assessment-intro.ptx" />

    <exercises>
        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The heights of Form 1 girls in a school are <m>X \sim N(158, 6)</m> cm.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(152 &lt; X &lt; 164)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>Find <m>P(X &gt; 170)</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p>How many girls taller than 170 cm would you expect in a class of 40?</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Here <m>\mu = 158</m>, <m>\sigma = 6</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>152 = \mu - \sigma</m>, <m>164 = \mu + \sigma</m>, so <m>P = 0.68</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>170 = \mu + 2\sigma</m>, so <m>P(X &gt; 170) = \frac{1-0.95}{2} = 0.025</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>0.025 \times 40 = 1</m> girl.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    The time <m>X</m> (in minutes) a customer waits at a bank counter follows an exponential distribution with <m>\lambda = 0.25</m> (mean = 4 minutes). Using <m>P(X &lt; x) = 1 - e^{-0.25x}</m>, find:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(X &lt; 4)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(X &gt; 8)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(X &lt; 4) = 1 - e^{-1} \approx 1 - 0.368 = 0.632</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(X &gt; 8) = e^{-0.25 \times 8} = e^{-2} \approx 0.135</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A dart is thrown randomly at a 30 cm × 30 cm square board. A circle of radius 10 cm is painted in the centre of the board. Assuming the dart always hits the board, find:
                </p>
                <figure xml:id="fig-dart-board">
                    <caption>The 30 cm × 30 cm square board (gray) with the circular target of radius 10 cm (blue) in the centre.</caption>
                    <image width="32%">
                        <latex-image>
                            \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=0.85]
                                % 1 unit = 5 cm; square is 6x6, circle radius 2
                                \fill[gray!20] (0,0) rectangle (6,6);
                                \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (6,6);
                                \fill[blue!25] (3,3) circle (2);
                                \draw[blue, thick] (3,3) circle (2);
                                % Labels
                                \node[blue!70!black, font=\small] at (3,3) {target};
                                \node[below, font=\scriptsize] at (3,-0.3) {30 cm};
                                \node[left, font=\scriptsize] at (-0.3,3) {30 cm};
                                % Radius marker
                                \draw[gray!70, thin] (3,3) -- (5,3);
                                \node[above, font=\scriptsize] at (4,3.1) {10 cm};
                                \fill (3,3) circle (0.07);
                            \end{tikzpicture}
                        </latex-image>
                    </image>
                </figure>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p>The probability the dart hits the circle.</p></li>
                        <li><p>The probability the dart misses the circle.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    Total area = <m>900 \text{ cm}^2</m>; circle area = <m>100\pi \text{ cm}^2</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(\text{hits}) = \dfrac{100\pi}{900} = \dfrac{\pi}{9} \approx 0.349</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(\text{misses}) = 1 - \dfrac{\pi}{9} \approx 0.651</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    A light bulb has a lifetime <m>X</m> (in hours) that follows an exponential distribution with <m>\lambda = 0.02</m> (mean = 50 hours). Using <m>P(X &lt; x) = 1 - e^{-0.02x}</m>, find:
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(X &lt; 20)</m></p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(X &gt; 100)</m></p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li><p><m>P(X &lt; 20) = 1 - e^{-0.4} \approx 1 - 0.670 = 0.330</m>.</p></li>
                        <li><p><m>P(X &gt; 100) = e^{-0.02 \times 100} = e^{-2} \approx 0.135</m>.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>

        <exercise>
            <statement>
                <p>
                    Kamau and Atieno agree to meet at a café between 12:00 and 13:00. Each arrives at a uniformly random time during that hour and waits at most 15 minutes before leaving. Let <m>X</m> and <m>Y</m> (in minutes past 12:00) be their arrival times. They meet if and only if <m>|X - Y| &lt; 15</m>.
                </p>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Their joint arrival time <m>(X, Y)</m> is a random point in a 60 × 60 square. Sketch the square and shade the region where they meet.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The region where they do NOT meet consists of two corner triangles above the line <m>Y = X + 15</m> and below <m>Y = X - 15</m>. Each triangle has legs of length 45. Find the total area of the non-meeting region.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li><p>Find the probability that Kamau and Atieno meet.</p></li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </statement>
            <answer>
                <p>
                    <ol marker="(a)">
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                The shaded meeting region is a hexagonal band across the diagonal of the 60 × 60 square, bounded by the lines <m>Y = X + 15</m> and <m>Y = X - 15</m>.
                            </p>
                            <figure xml:id="fig-meeting-problem">
                                <caption>
                                    The 60 × 60 square of arrival times <m>(X, Y)</m>. The blue band is the meeting region <m>|X - Y| &lt; 15</m>. The gray triangles are the non-meeting corners, each with legs of length 45 minutes.
                                </caption>
                                <image width="42%">
                                    <latex-image>
                                        \begin{tikzpicture}[scale=1.05]
                                            % 1 unit = 15 min; square is 4x4; threshold = 1 unit
                                            % Non-meeting triangles (gray)
                                            \fill[gray!35] (0,1) -- (0,4) -- (3,4) -- cycle;
                                            \fill[gray!35] (1,0) -- (4,0) -- (4,3) -- cycle;
                                            % Meeting region (blue hexagon)
                                            \fill[blue!20] (0,0) -- (1,0) -- (4,3) -- (4,4) -- (3,4) -- (0,1) -- cycle;
                                            % Square border
                                            \draw[thick] (0,0) rectangle (4,4);
                                            % Boundary lines
                                            \draw[dashed, blue!70] (0,1) -- (3,4);
                                            \draw[dashed, blue!70] (1,0) -- (4,3);
                                            % Diagonal Y=X for reference
                                            \draw[dotted, gray!60] (0,0) -- (4,4);
                                            % Axes
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (-0.2,0) -- (4.6,0) node[right] {$X$};
                                            \draw[-&gt;, thick] (0,-0.2) -- (0,4.6) node[above] {$Y$};
                                            % Tick marks and labels (1 unit = 15 min)
                                            \draw (1,0.06) -- (1,-0.06) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {15};
                                            \draw (2,0.06) -- (2,-0.06) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {30};
                                            \draw (3,0.06) -- (3,-0.06) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {45};
                                            \draw (4,0.06) -- (4,-0.06) node[below, font=\scriptsize] {60};
                                            \draw (0.06,1) -- (-0.06,1) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {15};
                                            \draw (0.06,2) -- (-0.06,2) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {30};
                                            \draw (0.06,3) -- (-0.06,3) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {45};
                                            \draw (0.06,4) -- (-0.06,4) node[left, font=\scriptsize] {60};
                                            % Region labels
                                            \node[blue!60!black, font=\scriptsize] at (2,2) {meet};
                                            \node[gray!70!black, font=\scriptsize] at (0.9,3.5) {miss};
                                            \node[gray!70!black, font=\scriptsize] at (3.1,0.5) {miss};
                                        \end{tikzpicture}
                                    </latex-image>
                                </image>
                            </figure>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Area of each triangle = <m>\frac{1}{2} \times 45 \times 45 = 1012.5</m>. Total non-meeting area = <m>2 \times 1012.5 = 2025</m>.
                            </p>
                        </li>
                        <li>
                            <p>
                                Total square area = <m>60 \times 60 = 3600</m>.
                                <md>P(\text{they meet}) = \frac{3600 - 2025}{3600} = \frac{1575}{3600} = \frac{7}{16} \approx 0.4375.</md>
                            </p>
                        </li>
                    </ol>
                </p>
            </answer>
        </exercise>
    </exercises>

